1
00:00:00,143 --> 00:00:02,917
{happy}Hello, and welcome to Shah Spectrum.

2
00:00:02,917 --> 00:00:07,407
Today's story starts on a small island with a very big number.

3
00:00:07,407 --> 00:00:11,086
{happy}Here is a number that sounds like a mistake.

4
00:00:11,086 --> 00:00:16,904
Singapore, a small island city, produced six point one per cent more in July to

5
00:00:16,904 --> 00:00:19,615
September than in the same three months a year earlier.

6
00:00:19,615 --> 00:00:22,507
So what is it selling that the world wants so much?

7
00:00:22,507 --> 00:00:28,837
{happy}I'm Mittal Shah, and today I'll show you what this tiny economy tells us

8
00:00:28,837 --> 00:00:31,508
about who gains when the world spends big on AI.

9
00:00:31,508 --> 00:00:32,196
{fluent}First, the numbers.

10
00:00:32,196 --> 00:00:38,629
On Tuesday, Singapore's Ministry of Energy, Trade and Industry published its figures

11
00:00:38,629 --> 00:00:40,261
for July to September.

12
00:00:40,261 --> 00:00:45,190
The economy grew six point one per cent from the same quarter last year.

13
00:00:45,190 --> 00:00:50,689
Compared with the previous three months, adjusted for seasonal patterns, it grew one

14
00:00:50,689 --> 00:00:51,906
point five per cent.

15
00:00:52,304 --> 00:00:54,123
That beat the first estimate.

16
00:00:54,123 --> 00:01:00,500
In October, the early figure was five point four per cent, based mostly on July and

17
00:01:00,500 --> 00:01:01,133
August data.

18
00:01:01,133 --> 00:01:02,401
Later data pushed it up.

19
00:01:02,401 --> 00:01:08,142
Over the first nine months, the economy is six point two per cent bigger than a year

20
00:01:08,142 --> 00:01:08,742
earlier.

21
00:01:08,635 --> 00:01:13,555
So the government raised its forecast for the whole of this year to around six per

22
00:01:13,555 --> 00:01:19,003
cent, up from a range of four and a half to five and a half.

23
00:01:19,394 --> 00:01:20,471
Now, what is behind the strength?

24
00:01:20,471 --> 00:01:21,505
The ministry points to AI.

25
00:01:21,505 --> 00:01:27,365
It says AI-related production and exports have kept accelerating since August,

26
00:01:27,365 --> 00:01:31,522
driven by a stronger-than-expected build-out of data centres.

27
00:01:31,973 --> 00:01:33,711
Here is how that works.

28
00:01:33,711 --> 00:01:37,391
A data centre is a huge building full of computers.

29
00:01:37,391 --> 00:01:43,950
Before anyone can switch one on, someone must make the electronic parts, move them

30
00:01:43,950 --> 00:01:44,549
and sell them.

31
00:01:44,480 --> 00:01:46,247
Singapore's figures show that work.

32
00:01:46,247 --> 00:01:53,124
Manufacturing grew eight point five per cent from a year earlier, led by electronics

33
00:01:53,124 --> 00:01:54,497
and precision engineering.

34
00:01:54,497 --> 00:01:59,780
And wholesale trade, which is firms selling to other businesses, grew fourteen point

35
00:01:59,780 --> 00:02:05,287
five per cent, led by firms selling machinery, telecom and computer equipment and

36
00:02:05,287 --> 00:02:08,040
electronic components.

37
00:02:08,577 --> 00:02:12,448
Now look at the other side of the same economy.

38
00:02:12,448 --> 00:02:16,276
Retail trade, which is shops selling to ordinary shoppers, fell two point seven per

39
00:02:16,276 --> 00:02:18,154
cent, also from a year earlier.

40
00:02:18,154 --> 00:02:24,332
Food and beverage services, which means restaurants and cafes, fell two point one

41
00:02:24,332 --> 00:02:24,932
per cent.

42
00:02:24,805 --> 00:02:26,297
So growth is uneven:

43
00:02:26,297 --> 00:02:31,576
strong in making and trading AI-related goods, negative in shops and restaurants.

44
00:02:31,976 --> 00:02:36,238
And the ministry expects a slower year in twenty twenty-seven.

45
00:02:36,238 --> 00:02:40,973
It forecasts growth of two and a half to four and a half per cent for twenty

46
00:02:40,973 --> 00:02:44,832
twenty-seven, and says growth is projected to moderate.

47
00:02:44,832 --> 00:02:49,566
It expects the pace of AI spending to ease from its high level this year.

48
00:02:49,959 --> 00:02:51,096
It also lists risks.

49
00:02:51,096 --> 00:02:55,713
First, concerns about whether the global AI cycle can last could trigger sharper

50
00:02:55,713 --> 00:02:57,760
corrections in financial markets.

51
00:02:57,760 --> 00:03:03,026
Second, further escalation of the conflict in the Middle East could bring fresh

52
00:03:03,026 --> 00:03:04,020
spikes in energy prices.

53
00:03:04,020 --> 00:03:10,398
Third, economic and political uncertainty in other economies could weigh on

54
00:03:10,398 --> 00:03:11,841
investment and spending.

55
00:03:12,248 --> 00:03:15,856
That first risk is the open question behind the whole boom.

56
00:03:15,856 --> 00:03:20,018
Will all the money going into AI chips, buildings and power pay off?

57
00:03:20,018 --> 00:03:25,130
Singapore is one place where the spending shows up as real growth today.

58
00:03:25,130 --> 00:03:28,604
It is also a place that would feel it if the spending slowed.

59
00:03:28,979 --> 00:03:30,420
So what do we not know?

60
00:03:30,420 --> 00:03:34,023
Nobody knows how long the data centre build-out will last.

61
00:03:34,023 --> 00:03:38,178
And one strong quarter does not tell us how the next ones will go.

62
00:03:38,178 --> 00:03:39,346
{happy}To sum up:

63
00:03:39,346 --> 00:03:44,414
AI-related demand for the parts Singapore makes and trades has lifted its growth

64
00:03:44,414 --> 00:03:45,014
this year.

65
00:03:44,886 --> 00:03:49,132
Its own ministry expects both growth and AI spending to ease next year.

66
